Sub-problem-Id: 2; Sub-problem: "What are the values of \( x \) that make the denominator \( x^2 + x - 6 \) equal to zero?"; Answer:

Since we have found the factored form of the denominator as $(x-\frac{1+\sqrt{13}}{2})(x-\frac{1-\sqrt{13}}{2})$, we can set it equal to zero and solve for $x$:

\begin{align*}
(x-\frac{1+\sqrt{13}}{2})(x-\frac{1-\sqrt{13}}{2})&=0\\
\Rightarrow\qquad x-\frac{1+\sqrt{13}}{2}&=0 \text{ or } x-\frac{1-\sqrt{13}}{2}=0\\
\Rightarrow\qquad x&=\frac{1+\sqrt{13}}{2} \text{ or } x=\frac{1-\sqrt{13}}{2}
\end{align*}

So, the values of $x$ that make the denominator equal to zero are $\boxed{\frac{1+\sqrt{13}}{2}, \frac{1-\sqrt{13}}{2}}$.
